# corral gate rules — installed to ~/.config/corral/rules, overridable with CORRAL_RULES.
# Comma-separated key=value. Missing gate keys are treated as empty (fail closed); missing or
# blank identity keys below fall back to their built-in defaults.
#
# `readonly` IS THE GATE BOUNDARY: an unpinned command whose first word is listed here runs
# with no lease and no record. List only subcommands that never touch a device. Adding a
# device-touching name (shell, install, push, pull, forward, reverse, root, remount, reboot,
# uninstall, emu, exec-out, logcat) disables the unpinned guard for it. `-s <serial>` and
# ANDROID_SERIAL are always lease-checked, whatever this file says.
readonly=devices,start-server,kill-server,track-devices,version,help
valueflags=-H,-P,-L,-p,-t

# Identity walk lists, shared by the CLI (corral gate / owner) and the bash wrapper.
# Membership ignores case AND one trailing `.exe` on BOTH sides, so write each program once:
# `bash` matches `bash.exe`, and the `foo`/`foo.exe` pairs below are harmless no-ops (a Linux
# binary literally named `bash.exe` would also be skipped — an accepted trade).
# shell_skip: processes that are never the agent — the walk steps over them (Unix + Windows).
# init_floor: the walk stops here and falls back to user:$USER (an immortal shared process
# must never become a principal). A wrong entry is silent and shared: the walk stops early and
# every session of that user collapses into one owner, with nothing in `corral owner` to show
# it — only add processes that truly sit above every agent. Unlike the gate keys above, absent
# or blank keys here fall back to built-in defaults: empty identity lists degrade attribution
# instead of failing closed.
shell_skip=bash,sh,zsh,fish,dash,ksh,env,nohup,timeout,xargs,sudo,setsid,stdbuf,script,cmd,cmd.exe,powershell,powershell.exe,pwsh,pwsh.exe,conhost,conhost.exe
init_floor=systemd,init,services,services.exe,wininit,wininit.exe,lsass,lsass.exe,svchost,svchost.exe,winlogon,winlogon.exe,system,explorer,explorer.exe
